From: mscharged-port
Subject: Pace and present frames from the host above the VI rate
Upstream-base: d664382f57002fbff143911c81b06b7e7a4b3ab4

The native VI presents at genuine retraces only: a retrace latches the
flushed XFB, commits it to the output and the owner's next service presents
it. A game that draws above the console's field rate needs each finished
frame shown as it swaps, while the retraces keep their real rate, counts and
callbacks for everything that counts them.

aurora_configure_video_pacing turns host pacing on with a frame slot (or no
time limit, where VSync alone holds presentation to the display). The game
then swaps through aurora_paced_video_swap instead of waiting for a retrace:
it commits the registers the next retrace would latch and presents them at
once, unless that flush also changes the TV mode or pan, which keeps the
retrace boundary. It then waits for the next slot in short sleeps that keep
servicing hardware interrupts, as VIWaitForRetrace does, with a final yield
loop for precision. A late frame starts the cadence again from now. A window
that presents nothing keeps at least the field period.

While pacing is on, the output skips a retrace commit that would show the
same completed copy with the same signal again; otherwise the latch of an
already presented frame would cost a second Present and, with VSync, a
display refresh. Without host pacing nothing changes.

